# Lesson 6, Mike's Exercise 4, turned so the student (South) is on lead.
title: Your Exercise 4: partner bid spades
id: L06-01
type: exercise
lesson: 6
concepts: 6.2, 6.1
student: S
board: 1

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  AJT73  J43    52      KQ6
  E  Q2     A5     AKJ876  932
  S  K5     T962   943     JT74
  W  9864   KQ87   QT      A85

auction:
  N Pass   = Not enough to open
  E 1D     = 13+ points, 4+ diamonds
  S Pass   = Not enough to bid
  W 1H     = 6+ points, 4+ hearts
  N 1S     = Spades: a good 5-card suit. Partner wants you to know about it.
  E 2D     = A minimum opening hand with 6 diamonds
  S Pass   = Nothing to add
  W 3D     = Diamond support, about 10-12 points: inviting game
  N Pass   = Nothing more to say
  E Pass   = A minimum: no game
  S Pass   = Nothing to add

auction recap:
  East opened 1♦ and West responded 1♥. Partner (North) then bid 1♠ to tell
  you about her spades. East's 2♦ showed a minimum with six diamonds, and
  West's 3♦ invited game, which East declined. East is declarer, because East
  bid diamonds first, and so you, South, will lead to the first trick.

lead planning:
  declarer: 13-15 = East opened, then rebid 2♦: a minimum opening hand, about 13 to 15.
  dummy: 10-12 = West's jump to 3♦ invites game: about 10 to 12.
  partner: 9-12 = 40, minus your 4, East's 13 to 15 and West's 10 to 12, leaves 9 to 13. North passed at first, so under 13.
  remind: Partner has a fair hand and bid spades. What does that suggest for your lead?

play:
  1   S SK*  W S4  N S3  E S2
      SK key: Partner bid spades, so lead them. With a doubleton honor, lead the honor: the king, then the 5.
      SK not S5: Right suit! With only two cards, lead the honor first. If you lead the 5 and partner wins the ace, your king is stuck alone.
      SK not H: Partner bid spades for a reason. Which suit did partner ask you to lead?
      SK not D: Leading declarer's trump suit rarely helps here. What did partner bid?
      SK not C: Clubs are a guess. Partner told you which suit she likes.
  2   S S5   W S6  N SA  E SQ
      note: Now the 5. Partner wins the ace, and declarer's queen falls.
  3   N CK   E C2  S C4  W CA
      note: Partner tries clubs next. Dummy's hearts could soon give declarer a club discard, so the defense must go after clubs now.
  4   W DQ   N D2  E D6  S D3
      note: Declarer draws trumps, starting with dummy's short side: queen and ten first.
  5   W DT   N D5  E D7  S D4
      note: Dummy's second trump.
  6   W H7   N H3  E HA  S H2
      note: Over to the ♥A in declarer's hand.
  7   E DA   S D9  W S8  N S7
      note: The last trump comes out.
  8   E H5   S H6  W HK  N H4
      note: Now to dummy's hearts.
  9   W HQ   N HJ  E C3  S H9
      note: Declarer throws a club on the ♥Q.
  10  W C5   N CQ  E C9  S C7
      note: Partner's ♣Q takes the defense's third trick.
  11  N ST   E D8  S CT  W S9
      note: Declarer ruffs, and the rest are trumps.
  12  E DK   S HT  W H8  N C6
  13  E DJ   S CJ  W C8  N SJ

play recap:
  Your ♠K went straight to the defense's best chance: partner's spades.
  The 5 next let partner win the ♠A, and then partner tried clubs. Declarer
  still made 10 tricks: dummy's ♥K and ♥Q took care of a club. No lead beats
  3♦ on this deal, but a lead in partner's suit is never the one you'll regret.
  If partner had held the ♠Q instead of the ace, your king would have forced
  out declarer's ace at once, leaving partner's queen ready to win later.
